{"seq":21,"at":"2026-11-11T14:06:58.449Z","type":"payment","job":"A","data":{"provider":"model-standard","step":"model-1","price":"0.020000","spent":"0.070000","status":200,"tries":2,"ms":13522,"invoice":"0x894d72ea999407a8d35a5aa11a2b745080c2e69becaef01bd6cf8be8101d0012:6c36aab4-220e-44e7-80cc-f3aee408a220"},"prev":"c7a64c932724be80037a6d42d37b685946dcaaead2febca1004797b36d692aa4","hash":"51a3edafbf846db0e1a7f2081e811b890bea7bac7bca73b1e4e444754a997995"}
{"seq":22,"at":"2026-11-11T14:06:58.450Z","type":"report.text","job":"A","data":{"step":"model-1","provider":"model-standard","text":"This report compares three ways regional grocers can cut cold-chain spoilage: sensor-based temperature monitoring, refrigeration equipment upgrades, and changes to delivery routes and inventory timing. The comparison matters because spoilage erodes thin grocery margins, and these options differ sharply in upfront cost, how fast they pay back, and how much operational change they demand. Each section weighs them on cost, expected payback, implementation effort, and fit for a mid-sized chain, so you can sequence investments sensibly."},"prev":"51a3edafbf846db0e1a7f2081e811b890bea7bac7bca73b1e4e444754a997995","hash":"59fa3e762fe8568f4921cb3c800a0e28d2251b674c73015502cbe9ad26e365e5"}
{"seq":23,"at":"2026-11-11T14:06:58.451Z","type":"step.upgraded","job":"A","data":{"paragraph":2,"provider":"model-standard","reason":"The middle paragraph most likely holds the head-to-head comparison of the three approaches, which is the core of what the report was asked to do."},"prev":"59fa3e762fe8568f4921cb3c800a0e28d2251b674c73015502cbe9ad26e365e5","hash":"92f7e10f0fb1400053e0c4c85707c8b684ba8dca3b78355e3195d834c30d5af0"}
{"seq":24,"at":"2026-11-11T14:07:04.568Z","type":"payment","job":"A","data":{"provider":"model-standard","step":"model-2","price":"0.020000","spent":"0.090000","status":200,"tries":1,"ms":6115,"invoice":"0x2166d9865a124a5048851b738e2bf516e2759e8b241851fd2b215103243aa2ed:e3dfd646-170a-4376-941a-95d98042b5aa"},"prev":"92f7e10f0fb1400053e0c4c85707c8b684ba8dca3b78355e3195d834c30d5af0","hash":"078ea670a7a0adf2cce28618c04aa8cdd29934a381899c98e6653d25c88f24d0"}
{"seq":25,"at":"2026-11-11T14:07:04.569Z","type":"report.text","job":"A","data":{"step":"model-2","provider":"model-standard","text":"Sensor-based monitoring is usually the cheapest of the three to start, because it adds visibility rather than changing physical assets. Hardware per store, subscription fees, and staff time for responding to alerts make up most of the cost. Its benefit is indirect: it catches failing compressors, door-open events, and receiving-dock lapses before product is lost. To judge payback, compare write-offs tied to temperature events before and after installation, not total shrink."},"prev":"078ea670a7a0adf2cce28618c04aa8cdd29934a381899c98e6653d25c88f24d0","hash":"25579426e6364987a3f599b4d9cb237da1f6842767099926f4cc7ba250f118f0"}
{"seq":26,"at":"2026-11-11T14:07:04.569Z","type":"step.upgraded","job":"A","data":{"paragraph":3,"provider":"model-standard","reason":"The conclusion carries the recommendation, the part readers act on, so it gets the most scrutiny."},"prev":"25579426e6364987a3f599b4d9cb237da1f6842767099926f4cc7ba250f118f0","hash":"90fdbc75cfdaa9492c379b6335d20c52280621f4a8be74c3d30b37b0f1d4ff99"}
{"seq":27,"at":"2026-11-11T14:07:18.674Z","type":"payment","job":"A","data":{"provider":"model-standard","step":"model-3","price":"0.020000","spent":"0.110000","status":200,"tries":3,"ms":14104,"invoice":"0x483611b234b1fb85725a8d166fe72dba989873a9954d84f4d2574d39bc4f9d87:e17d458d-354a-40e8-a5a6-4682fdf17cd7"},"prev":"90fdbc75cfdaa9492c379b6335d20c52280621f4a8be74c3d30b37b0f1d4ff99","hash":"5e2d1e8992702cf4558f299673dcce159d66401636faca1fe25daf4bf21eabaf"}
{"seq":28,"at":"2026-11-11T14:07:18.676Z","type":"report.text","job":"A","data":{"step":"model-3","provider":"model-standard","text":"For most regional grocers, the sensible sequence is to start with monitoring, then use its data to decide where the other two approaches belong. Alert logs will show which stores suffer equipment failures, which suffer handling lapses, and which receive product already warmed in transit. Stores with repeated compressor or case faults justify equipment upgrades; chronic delivery-related excursions justify route and timing changes. Before committing capital, measure temperature-related write-offs per store and track them for a full seasonal cycle."},"prev":"5e2d1e8992702cf4558f299673dcce159d66401636faca1fe25daf4bf21eabaf","hash":"7dce3133d6136b1947ffe8038cc94baf1c12ff19d57137724cf29a116e19149e"}
